import json

V = []

def rec(mid, verdict, trace, corrected, hardening, scaling, xtenant):
    V.append({
        "MECHANISM_ID": mid,
        "VERDICT": verdict,
        "ATTACK_TRACE": trace,
        "CORRECTED_INVARIANT": corrected,
        "HARDENING_REQUIRED": hardening,
        "SCALING_RISK": scaling,
        "CROSS_TENANT_RISK": xtenant,
    })

rec("O-1", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Worker W1 starts run R; the SQLite row goes to status running. W1 is SIGKILLed mid-run. The record admits there is no heartbeat or lease, so the row stays running forever. Recovery spawns W2, which reads the row and resumes the worktree. W1 was not actually dead (network partition, or slow shutdown) and also resumes. Both workers execute the same side-effecting steps; the status-transition-only path has no claim or CAS, so the second claim silently succeeds and the side effects double. Separately, the row persists the raw prompt (ASSUMED: prompts may carry secrets); the record's security model admits any process with file access can read and mutate runs, and there is no tenant column, so one per-host file holds every tenant's prompts and run state.",
 "Every unit of agent execution has exactly one durable identity row before it starts; status transitions are the only mutation path; the row carries a tenant id and a lease (holder id, expiry, fencing token); claiming or resuming a running row requires a fencing-token CAS; the persisted prompt excludes secret material (redacted or by reference).",
 "Add lease plus expiry plus fencing token to the run row; claim and resume via CAS; mandatory tenant_id; redact secrets out of the persisted prompt or store references. In CRX, implement as a run-scoped namespace of the PG journal, not a second SQLite writer (the record's own integration note says this).",
 "1k concurrent tasks: single-writer SQLite with SetMaxOpenConns(1) serializes all status churn; busy_timeout converts contention into latency. No multi-machine story: the file is per-host, so a worker pool spread across hosts cannot share it. 1k tenants: no tenant column and no access control on the file.",
 "yes: runs for all tenants share one file with no tenant field; any local process with file access reads every tenant's prompts and run state.")

rec("oa-tool-invocation-identity", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Retry after UNKNOWN: worker W1 executes send_invoice(amount=100.00); the provider accepts it but W1 dies before the receipt. The coordinator retries on W2. The fingerprint hashes type, name, namespace, server_label, arguments, environment, and caller. W2 runs under a different environment tag (ASSUMED: pool workers carry distinct environment tags), so the retry hashes differently, the ledger finds no match, and the invoice is sent twice: the fingerprint is not retry-stable across workers. Separately, when arguments fail JSON parsing, the normalizer falls back to hashing the raw string, so two whitespace variants of identical semantics produce different fingerprints and duplicate detection silently fails. The library invariant (identical semantics implies identical fingerprint regardless of whitespace) therefore holds only for JSON-parseable arguments of recognized types.",
 "Two invocations with identical normalized (type, name, namespace, server_label, arguments) produce identical fingerprints regardless of JSON key order or whitespace, for recognized types with JSON-parseable arguments; unrecognized or unparseable arguments are hashed opaquely (whitespace-sensitive) or return None. The fingerprint is an exactness key, not a retry key: retry-stable dedupe requires a separate idempotency scope that excludes environment and caller, or an explicit caller-supplied idempotency key.",
 "Define a distinct idempotency key (semantic fingerprint minus environment and caller, or an explicit idempotency_key) for cross-worker retry dedupe; keep the full fingerprint for exactness. Bind tenant into the fingerprint scope; key ledger lookups by (tenant, idempotency key).",
 "Negligible: O(payload) SHA-256, stateless pure function, thread-safe. 1k tasks and 1k tenants add no coordination cost.",
 "yes if adopted verbatim: identical semantics across tenants produce identical fingerprints, so a globally keyed dedupe ledger could confuse tenant B's call with tenant A's record. No if keyed by (tenant, key).")

rec("oa-tool-invocation-ledger", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Crash between _mark_tool_invocation_executed and the actual provider dispatch: on restore the ledger shows executed=True, completed=False. The flag name says executed, so recovery (or an operator) reads it as the tool ran and either skips the step (lost work) or re-runs it (duplicate side effect). The ledger records the planning transition, not the side-effect boundary, and defines no recovery classification; the openai-agents-sdk lane falsified SDK durability as a native property (RunState is pause/resume, not crash recovery). Manufactured success: _mark_tool_call_completed is invoked by the worker itself, so a compromised worker records completed=True with fabricated output and the ledger permanently vouches for it; the lane notes the SDK has no signature, witness, or hash concept. Replay: a stale worker re-sends an old provider call ID with new output; completions are keyed by call ID with no write-once rule stated, so the fabricated result can overwrite the real one.",
 "planned, dispatched, and completed are distinct lifecycle flags: dispatched=True requires the provider or transport ack at the true side-effect boundary; completed=True implies a receipt was recorded by the claimant and is labeled unverified-claim until independently evidenced; completion entries are write-once per call ID; on recovery, planned-and-not-dispatched is safe to retry while dispatched-and-not-completed is OutcomeUnknown, never silently re-executed or skipped.",
 "Split executed into planned/dispatched with the dispatch marker at the provider ack; define recovery classification per flag combination; make completion entries write-once; grade receipts verified vs claimed; extend the duplicate call-ID loud failure to same-run replays.",
 "Low: run-local dict, O(n) serialization. 1k concurrent runs are independent; no shared locks.",
 "no, provided the ledger stays run-scoped and run IDs are tenant-bound; the record's nested-run UserError already rejects cross-run call-ID merging.")

rec("orka-write-once-execution-binding", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Poisoned minting: the coordinator is compromised at dispatch and mints a binding routing the attempt to an attacker-controlled runtime with an elevated agent profile. Write-once immutability then locks the attack in: every worker action validates against the frozen binding and passes, and recovery re-validates the same poisoned binding. The record's security model covers only post-dispatch compromise, not malicious creation. Duplicate holders: a dispatch is delivered twice (fan-out bug); two workers both present the same valid binding and both execute side effects, because the binding is validated but never leased to a single holder. Stale binding: a binding minted at t0 with an authorization snapshot is acted on at t1 after the grant was revoked; nothing re-checks freshness, so the revocation is bypassed.",
 "One binding per attempt, immutable once created, created only under a recorded authorization with input digests (binding creation is itself a canonical event with provenance); the binding carries a freshness bound (policy and config versions, valid-until) and a single-holder lease with fencing token; every worker action is validated against the frozen binding AND the holder lease AND the freshness bound, never against live coordinator state.",
 "Authorize and evidence binding creation; add a freshness bound with commit-time re-verification; single-holder lease with fencing token on dispatch; store credential references or digests in the binding, never secret material; bind tenant into the binding identity.",
 "Low: small immutable records, write-once, read-heavy. 1k tasks and 1k tenants scale with attempt count; validators do O(1) checks per action.",
 "no if namespace/tenant is frozen in the binding and validators enforce it; yes if the binding identity omits tenant, since the record does not explicitly require it.")

rec("RESTATE-INVOCATION-ID", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Bearer attach: the record states knowledge of an ID grants attach/output access. If CRX adopts attach-by-ID rendezvous and partition keys are predictable (tenant name, sequential mission numbers), tenant B guesses tenant A's invocation IDs, attaches to A's executions, and reads outputs. ID-only singularity: two workers act under one invocation ID after a duplicate dispatch; the ID format does nothing to stop the second execution. One invocation ID names one logical execution only if the journal enforces it; the record's invariant states the aspiration, not the enforcement. (License note: BSL-1.1, pattern only, do not vendor code.)",
 "One invocation ID (mission ID, attempt sequence, UUIDv7) names exactly one logical execution across retries and recoveries; singularity is enforced by a journal unique constraint on (invocation ID, step key), the ID merely makes the key stable and routable; IDs are never capabilities: attach and output rendezvous require an independent authorization check.",
 "Pair the ID shape with journal unique constraints; never expose bearer attach without authorization; embed tenant in the routing key.",
 "Negligible: ID generation is local and O(1); embedded routing key avoids lookups. 1k tasks and 1k tenants are fine.",
 "yes if partition keys are predictable and attach-by-ID is exposed; no if IDs are unguessable, tenant-keyed, and attach requires authorization.")

rec("orka-outcome-unknown-fail-closed", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Boundary misclassification: a worker's request bytes leave the socket but the process dies before the transport ack is processed locally; the local state machine still reads Submitting, which the mechanism classifies as pre-boundary and safe to requeue. Recovery re-submits; the provider executes twice (double charge, double email). The mechanism's correctness hinges on the boundary being externally observable, but the record defines it by local state-machine position. Evidence-free unknown: a compromised coordinator marks an inconvenient failed attempt as OutcomeUnknown with no cited evidence, laundering a failure into an honest-looking terminal state.",
 "Ambiguity is a terminal, explicitly named state, never an absence of state. 'May have been accepted' is determined by the last externally observable boundary evidence (provider or transport ack), never by local state-machine position; the OutcomeUnknown record cites that evidence. A prompt past a boundary that cannot be proven uncrossed is never re-submitted by recovery.",
 "Define every side-effect boundary by externally observable ack, recorded per step with its effect class; OutcomeUnknown records must cite last-observed evidence; enforce no-replay in the recovery path and cover it with a journal check.",
 "Low: recovery is a bounded classification pass over stale attempts; no retry amplification.",
 "no: classification is per-attempt and carries no cross-tenant data.")

rec("DBOS-STEP-RECORDING", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Residual window: a worker executes a non-transactional external call (payment API) and crashes before the step record commits. Recovery replays the unrecorded suffix and the payment executes twice. The record honestly admits this window, but the harvested invariant ('Recorded = done, never re-executed') overclaims for external effects, where recorded means claimed-done. Manufactured output: a compromised worker writes a fabricated step output to the system DB; recovery returns the stored fabrication forever, since step records are authoritative and carry no receipt verification. Writer spoofing: a worker writes a completed step record for a step owned by another attempt, causing recovery to skip work that never ran.",
 "For transactional effects, the step record and the effects commit atomically: recorded = done, never re-executed. For external effects, the record is a claim: recorded = claimed-done, re-execution is bounded to the unrecorded suffix, the step is labeled with its effect class (transactional, idempotent-external, at-least-once-external), and at-least-once steps carry provider idempotency keys so re-execution is safe. Step records are written only by the step owner's identity; external-effect outputs carry provider receipts or are labeled unverified-claim.",
 "Per-step effect-class labels; provider idempotency keys on external steps; writer-identity enforcement on step records; receipt verification or claim labeling on stored outputs.",
 "Moderate: one PG row per step; 1k concurrent workflows need indexed (workflow, step) keys. Fine for PG at this scale.",
 "no if step rows are tenant-scoped; the record assumes a trusted system DB, so tenant scoping must be explicit.")

rec("oa-approval-scope-fingerprint", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Argument smuggling: an always_approve grant is cached for scope (send_email). A rogue worker invokes send_email with a different recipient and exfiltrated content; the scope fingerprint is unchanged because arguments are deliberately excluded, so the cached grant authorizes it. The library admits the tradeoff, but its invariant ('same scope fingerprint implies same authorization target') is false: same scope, different authority implications. The openai-agents-sdk lane independently qualified this: approvals authorize invocations at call scope, not actions, and there is no record of what the invocation did. Revocation bypass: admin revokes send_email for the agent at t1 under policy P1; the agent retries a call approved at t0; the cached always_approve grant carries no policy-version binding, so it still applies under P1.",
 "Same scope fingerprint implies same tool target (type, role, lookup key); it does not imply same argument-level authority or same policy version. Grants are tiered: scope-level grants for low-risk tools, argument-bound grants (semantic fingerprint or allowlist constraints) for sensitive tools; every cached grant binds the policy and config version it was issued under and is re-validated at execution time.",
 "Two-tier grants (scope vs argument-bound) with per-tool policy; policy-version binding on cached grants plus execution-time re-check against current policy (M26 discipline); tenant in the scope fingerprint.",
 "Negligible: same cost as the identity fingerprint, cached per run.",
 "yes if scope omits tenant: identical tool scopes across tenants share cached grants. No with tenant in the scope key.")

rec("LANGGRAPH-INTERRUPT", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Self-approval: interrupt I1 (approve a wire transfer) is raised with no bound responder. The attacker, able to submit resume commands, sends Command(resume={I1: yes}); the checkpoint accepts it because the record trusts the answer (its security model states none built-in). Checkpoint fork: two coordinators resume from the same checkpoint after a crash; both reproduce I1; two different humans answer; both branches proceed and the transfer executes twice, because resume is not single-writer. Approval drift: the approver sees the interrupt payload at t0; the tool arguments change before execution at t1; the approval is honored for arguments the approver never saw, because the interrupt record does not bind the approved semantic fingerprint. (ASSUMED: interrupt IDs could be predictable; they must be unguessable.)",
 "Interrupts are values in the checkpoint, not control flow; each interrupt binds the authorized responder identity, the exact payload shown, and the semantic fingerprint of the proposed action; resume is a data command carrying responder provenance and is a single-writer CAS on the checkpoint version; execution validates the action still matches the approved fingerprint and the policy version current at approval; replaying the checkpoint reproduces the interrupt instead of re-asking.",
 "Responder-identity binding on interrupts; provenance on resume commands; single-writer resume CAS; approved-fingerprint binding between interrupt and execution; policy-version binding; unguessable interrupt IDs. Do not inherit the trusted-answer model: in CRX the answer becomes an evidence event (who answered, what they saw, schema validation).",
 "Low: in-checkpoint, no extra infra. 1k concurrent interrupts are independent checkpoint rows.",
 "no if interrupt and checkpoint rows are tenant-scoped; yes if resume commands can address interrupts across tenant boundaries, so the resume path must enforce tenant.")

rec("MCP-M26", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Revocation race: admin revokes the tool grant at t1; a request that passed the execution re-check at t0 starts its handler at t2 and executes without authority. The record admits the time-of-check race. Long-handler drift: a 20-minute handler starts authorized; the grant is revoked at minute 5; nothing re-checks mid-execution. STDIO inheritance: CRX adopts the pattern including the documented STDIO exemption (skip auth for local processes); a compromised local worker then invokes any tool with no auth context, because 'local' was treated as trusted. Forged context: the re-check resolves the access context from caller-supplied claims rather than a trusted authenticator (ASSUMED deployment gap; the MCP lane notes audience and issuer enforcement is 'up to the verifier' and default deployments do not cryptographically verify), so a malicious caller mints its own authority.",
 "Authorization is checked at discovery AND at execution against a context minted by the authentication layer and bound to the transport session, never caller-asserted; missing context denies; forbidden tools are indistinguishable from absent ones; grants are epoch or lease-bound and long-running handlers re-verify; no local-process exemption exists in CRX: every tool call carries an auth context.",
 "Lease or epoch-bound grants with handler-start binding and periodic re-verification for long handlers; auth context minted by the authenticator and session-bound; no STDIO-style exemption; ambiguous denial preserved; log the grant epoch on every execution for audit.",
 "Low: two auth checks per call, context resolution cached per request. 1k tenants fine with per-request caching.",
 "no if the access context carries tenant and the checks enforce it; the middleware shape supports this but the record does not state it, so it must be explicit.")

rec("MCP-M04", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Unconfirmed cancel: the caller abandons a payment tool call; notifications/cancelled is emitted but lost on a dead transport (the record admits best-effort delivery). The server never sees it, completes the payment, and the caller records CANCELLED. The journal now shows CANCELLED while the world shows the payment happened: an unrecorded side effect, the exact failure the cancel path was meant to prevent. Retry-after-abandon: the caller abandons, immediately retries as a new call, and the abandoned handler also completes; the payment executes twice. The MCP lane found no idempotency keys anywhere in SDK v2, so nothing makes the retry safe.",
 "Every abandonment emits a cancellation signal (best-effort); signal delivery and handler termination are not guaranteed. CANCELLED is a terminal state only on server-confirmed cancellation or proof the handler never started; unconfirmed cancellations settle to OutcomeUnknown. Tool calls carry idempotency keys so retry-after-abandon is safe.",
 "Server-confirmed cancellation before CANCELLED; unconfirmed abandonment settles to OutcomeUnknown with the last-observed evidence; idempotency keys on all side-effecting tool calls; CANCELLED as a first-class terminal state with a causation link to the abandoning event.",
 "Negligible: O(1) per request.",
 "no.")

rec("MCP-M06", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Hung handler: a tool handler deadlocks waiting on a downstream that never responds. The client's read_timeout fires (no data flowing), the caller records TIMED_OUT and moves on, but the server-side handler has no execution bound (the record admits none was found in SDK v2) and holds the session forever. Repeated across tenants, hung handlers exhaust the session pool: a noisy-neighbor denial of service that no read or idle timeout prevents, because the timeouts cover reads and idleness, not execution. The library invariant ('every wait has a bound') is factually wrong on this point.",
 "Every network and idle wait has a bound, and disabling a bound is an explicit, logged opt-out. Tool handler execution time is NOT bounded by the SDK and must be bounded by the host: the worker enforces an execution budget and kills over-budget handlers, emitting a canonical TIMED_OUT event with causation.",
 "Host-enforced execution budget (worker watchdog or execution lease with kill) in addition to the SDK's read/idle timeouts; TIMED_OUT as a terminal state distinct from FAILED with the budget recorded on the call; explicit disables flagged in config review.",
 "Moderate without the host bound: hung handlers pin workers and sessions; at 1k concurrent tasks a handful of hung handlers can exhaust the pool. With the host bound, negligible.",
 "yes without the host bound: one tenant's hung handler consumes shared session and worker capacity (noisy neighbor). No with per-tenant execution budgets and pool isolation.")

rec("orka-single-control-authority", "SURVIVES",
 "Attempted second authority: a developer adds an outbox table with its own writer for performance. The invariant forbids it: the table may only commit inside the authority's fenced transaction carrying the controller epoch and session fence; there is no independent commit path, so the 'optimization' either goes through the fence or fails closed. Key loss: the snapshot encryption key is lost; startup fails closed and loud instead of minting a new key and silently orphaning snapshots. Both attacks are defeated by construction, not by detection.",
 "",
 "None as a mechanism. One assumption must hold: the controller epoch and session fences are anchored in a lease or consensus external to the fenced stores (K8s leader election in Orka; the PG journal's single-writer discipline in CRX). If the epoch lives in the same store it fences, the guarantee is circular.",
 "Low: auxiliary stores scale as read models; they never need independent consensus. 1k tasks and 1k tenants add read load only.",
 "no: the invariant is about write authority, and tenant scoping rides on the authority's own schema.")

rec("BORS-PREFLIGHT-MERGE-GATE", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Batch-window drift: patch P passes preflight at assembly (approvals bound to head SHA abc123, checks green). The batch CI run takes 40 minutes. During the run the author force-pushes (head moves to def456) and a required check's configuration changes. The batch lands because the gate was evaluated at assembly time and the record states no land-time re-verification; untested code merges under a stale approval. Queue bypass: a maintainer pushes directly to the target ref, bypassing the serial queue entirely; the preflight checklist is moot because the queue was never the sole write path.",
 "Approval is meaningful only when bound to the exact commit being merged; required checks must be present and green at gate time AND re-verified at land time (CAS on the target ref: land only if the ref still equals the SHA the batch was built and tested on); the queue is effective only if it is the sole write path to the target ref (branch protection at the hosting layer).",
 "Land-time SHA re-verification (CAS on target ref); sole-write-path enforcement via branch protection; every unmet condition remains a distinct typed block event.",
 "Low: preflight is per-patch at assembly; land-time re-verification is an O(1) ref comparison.",
 "not applicable in the bors context; in CRX-analog terms (candidate integration trees), tenant scoping rides on the journal.")

rec("BORS-HEAD-RACE-DETECTION", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Same batch-window drift as preflight: the head-SHA comparison runs at batch assembly; a head move during the batch CI window is not re-checked at land time, so :race detection covers assembly but not landing. Two batchers: a misconfigured deployment runs two batcher instances; both assemble batches from the same queue and both pass race detection against the same heads, then interleave staging pushes. The one-writer property comes from single deployment, not from the mechanism; the record's invariant does not survive a second writer.",
 "A patch is batchable only against the exact head SHA reviewed and tested; head movement is detected before batch assembly (:race, retryable, never conflated with :conflict) AND re-verified at land time; batch assembly is single-writer (one batcher holds the queue lease), so the SHA comparison is meaningful.",
 "Land-time SHA re-verification; single-writer lease on the batch queue; :race vs :conflict distinction preserved through the land path.",
 "Low: per-batch O(patches) at assembly.",
 "not applicable.")

rec("ORCA-14", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Silent watcher death: the lane completes at 17:01 and enqueues the durable wakeup event; the polling watcher was SIGKILLed at 16:58. The record admits a killed watcher silently stops wakeups. The durable queue holds the event but nothing drains it; the orchestrator never wakes; settlement and follow-up never fire. The incident the pattern was meant to fix recurs one layer down: durability without a live delivery path. Poll-latency miss: the watcher polls every 60 seconds; an approval gate with a 30-second expiry lapses before the orchestrator wakes, and the mission fails for want of a timely wakeup. Restart duplicates: the watcher restarts with no durable cursor (ASSUMED: the record states none) and re-prints the last transition; the orchestrator double-handles the terminal transition (double settlement).",
 "Every terminal transition is durably queued AND delivered over a live path with its own liveness proof: the watcher runs supervised (restart on death), carries a durable cursor (exactly-once delivery per transition), and its poll interval is shorter than the shortest lease or approval window it serves. Queue durability is necessary but never sufficient: a durable queue without a live, supervised delivery path is a liveness bug.",
 "Supervised watcher (or push-with-ack delivery with redelivery instead of poll-and-print); durable delivery cursor; wakeup-latency SLA bound to lease and approval windows; idempotent consumers; multiplex one watcher per coordinator over N lanes instead of one poller per orchestrator at scale.",
 "Moderate as specified: one poll per interval per orchestrator; at 1k concurrent missions the lane.list read projection takes 1k times the polling load. Multiplexing or push fan-out is required.",
 "yes if one watcher multiplexes tenants: wake lines must be tenant-scoped and the watcher must not leak one tenant's lane transitions to another orchestrator. No with per-tenant watch credentials and tenant-bound queues.")

rec("LETTA-GIT-MEMORY", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Silent degradation: the git repo becomes unavailable (disk full). The manager degrades to non-versioned writes without failing; corrections now destructively overwrite history with no alarm, and the correction-survival guarantee evaporates exactly under stress. Poison versioning: a prompt-injected agent writes attacker content to a memory block; git faithfully versions the poison (append-only history is not a trust gate). Secret permanence: a missed secret lands in a block while the redactor runs elsewhere or not at all; the secret is now in immutable git history with no destructive rewrite path, so it can never be purged and every clone carries it. Concurrent writers: two agents write the same block; merge conflicts and PG cache races are unaddressed by the record.",
 "Every memory state is recoverable by commit; the cache never invents a state git does not have; git unavailability fails correction writes closed (or queues them) with a loud typed event, never silently degrades to destructive writes; every commit carries provenance (causation id, source event); redaction runs before the git write; per-block writes are serialized by CAS on the expected commit.",
 "Fail-closed (or queued) behavior on git unavailability with loud alerting; provenance metadata on commits; redaction gate before the git write; defined secret-purge procedure under dual control for the inevitable miss; per-block write CAS; tenant-scoped repos or branches with hosting-layer access control (git has no native per-tenant ACLs).",
 "Moderate: git history grows linearly with edits; the PG cache keeps reads fast. 1k tenants need tenant-scoped repos, or the history becomes one giant shared log.",
 "yes as specified: one repo with no tenant ACLs means any reader sees every tenant's memory history. No with tenant-scoped repos or branches plus access control.")

rec("GRAPHITI-BITEMPORAL-INVALIDATE", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Poisoned supersession: the attacker plants a false refund policy in tool output; the extraction pipeline creates edge E2 with valid_at tomorrow. resolve_edge_contradictions applies pure timestamp logic: E2.valid_at is greater than E1.valid_at, so the true edge E1 is stamped invalid and the lie becomes current truth. The mechanism's determinism is not correctness: it deterministically launders poison, and the record's security model ('invalidation requires a contradicting edge from the same pipeline') assumes the pipeline is trusted while CRX's proven gap is that untrusted content reaches memory. Future-dated valid_at: the attacker asserts valid_at far in the future; timestamp logic crowns it. Silent skip: two contradictory edges with overlapping windows hit the skip branch and both remain 'current'; consumers see an unresolved contradiction with no conflict event.",
 "Supersession is append-only AND trust-gated: a contradicting edge supersedes only with provenance trust at least equal to the edge it supersedes and with evidenced valid_at (un-evidenced valid_at defaults to ingestion time, never the future); otherwise it is recorded as a contested candidate. Contradiction resolves to timestamp stamps, never to mutation or deletion of the old edge; skipped overlaps emit a typed conflict event for resolution.",
 "Trust-gated invalidation (provenance trust comparison; human or verified confirmation for high-trust edges); evidenced valid_at with no future dating; typed conflict events on skipped overlaps. Note: bi-temporal invalidation never deletes, so a separate erasure path is needed for secret purge and data-rights requests.",
 "Low: per-ingestion cost bounded by candidate edges between the same node pair plus hybrid-search candidates.",
 "no if edges are tenant-scoped and contradiction candidates are restricted to the same tenant's edges; yes if the candidate search crosses tenants, letting one tenant's content invalidate another's truth.")

rec("MEM0-EXPIRATION", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Fail-open immortality: a row's expiration_date becomes unparseable (manual DB edit, writer bypassing validation). The record treats unparseable stored dates as not-expired, so the row stays visible forever; the forgetting guarantee is void for exactly the rows most likely to be adversarial, and 'conservative' here means fail-open on the forget path. Writer-controlled soft delete: a malicious writer sets expiration_date to yesterday on another writer's true memory; the truth disappears from default reads with no authority check: a deletion primitive disguised as expiration. Conversely, poison gets expiration_date 2099 and persists.",
 "A memory past its expiration_date is invisible to default reads and the row is retained for audit; any row whose expiration_date cannot be parsed is treated as expired (fail-closed) and quarantined with a typed event; expiration changes are versioned events with writer identity, and shortening another writer's expiration requires explicit authority.",
 "Fail-closed on unparseable stored dates (quarantine plus typed event); versioned expiration changes; authority rule for shortening others' expirations. Note: read-time evaluation does not retract already-retrieved content from agent context.",
 "Negligible: O(1) date comparison per row at read and filter time.",
 "no if rows are tenant-scoped; the expiration logic itself is tenant-neutral.")

rec("MEMORYBANK-EBBINGHAUS", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Evidence destruction: a fraud-evidence memory goes unrecalled for months; the load-time sweep's coin flip deletes it from the JSON bank. The deletion is destructive (pop, not tombstone), so audit and replay can never recover it; applied to anything evidence-grade, this violates CRX's evidence rules. Reinforcement laundering: an attacker (or a looping agent) repeatedly retrieves a poisoned memory; each retrieval bumps strength and resets last_recall, so the poison becomes the strongest, longest-lived record in the bank: retrieval-as-consolidation amplifies the poison it should forget. Writer-set importance: the writer sets maximum initial importance on poison, flattening its decay curve. Nondeterminism: the load-time coin flip makes 'what exists' depend on unlogged randomness, violating CRX's determinism rule for constitutional outputs.",
 "Retention is a deterministic function of (time-since-recall, strength); decay applies only to derived-cache memories, never to evidence-grade or provenance records, and deletion is a tombstone (recoverable, auditable), never a destructive pop; reinforcement happens only through retrieval in verified contexts and never on mere surfacing; initial strength is capped unless evidenced; any randomness is seeded and logged for replay.",
 "Scope decay to derived cache; tombstone instead of delete; trust-gated reinforcement; capped initial strength; deterministic or replayable-logged decay decisions. Without these, the mechanism is a liability to CRX's evidence model, not an asset.",
 "Low: O(memories) scan at load; no background process. At 1k tenants the per-bank scan stays per-tenant.",
 "no: per-user banks are the record's stated isolation, and it holds if bank paths are tenant-bound and never cross-read.")

rec("CHAMNAN-REDACTOR", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Novel shape miss: a secret arrives with a new key prefix (sk-new-...) or split across lines or inside a base64 blob; the regex set has no pattern for it, the measured recall was computed on a synthetic corpus that never included the shape, and the secret persists into memory, then into prompts and exports. The library honestly documents its ceiling, but adoption as the sole gate treats a measured speed bump as a boundary. Ingress bypass: a memory enters through the cache-sync or import path (compare LETTA's sync_blocks_from_git) that does not call the ingestion function; the invariant ('no string enters memory without passing the redactor') holds only for the one code path, not the store. Stale corpus: new secret formats appear after the corpus was built; recall silently decays with no regression signal.",
 "No string enters the memory store through any ingress (API, sync, import, restore, backfill) without passing the redactor; redactor quality is measured on a corpus that includes real-shape and adversarial variants (split, encoded, prefixed), and recall is re-measured on a schedule with regression alerting; the redactor is one layer in a defense-in-depth (structured secret passing by reference, output-side scanning before external projection), never the sole gate.",
 "Enforce redaction at every ingress path, not just the main ingestion function; expand the corpus to real-shape plus adversarial variants and re-measure on a schedule; one-time backfill scan of existing memories plus periodic re-scans; pair with by-reference secret passing and output-side scanning.",
 "Low: regex over content length; per-ingress cost only.",
 "no: redaction is tenant-neutral string processing; ensure the corpus and logs do not themselves become a secret store.")

rec("OBS-8CHECKS", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Detective-only bypass: a compromised worker executes a tool call and simply never emits the execution-start event; the execution-without-authorization anti-join finds no execution event and reports nothing, because the check detects unauthorized events, not unrecorded executions. Absence of evidence is invisible. Forged authorization: the worker emits its own authorization receipt event citing a fabricated grant; the anti-join matches execution to receipt and passes, because receipts are verified for existence, not authorizer provenance. Paraphrase exfiltration: the model uses retrieved memory without citing memory IDs; the memory-use-without-retrieval set difference finds no cited IDs and passes. Paperwork-complete fabrication: the worker emits a mutation event citing a receipt ID for a receipt event it also fabricated; the mutation-without-receipt check verifies the receipt exists, not that the world changed. All four checks prove the paperwork is complete, never that the claims are true. Preconditions missing: the CRX baseline states mission lifecycle events lack causation_id and the observability classes and tables are unwired and empty, so several checks have nothing to join on today.",
 "The eight checks are sound as detective backstops over the canonical journal and necessary as specifications, but they are not sufficient as the sole control: authorization and duplicate-execution checks must also exist as preventive gates (dispatch-time authorization re-check; unique constraint on the deterministic execution key, not just a detective query); authorization receipts are emitted by the authorization authority, never by the authorized party; execution-start events are emitted by the dispatcher, not the worker; receipts pin policy, config, and snapshot versions so the stale-snapshot check is non-vacuous; external-effect receipts carry independent evidence or are labeled claims.",
 "Preventive form wherever the journal supports it (unique constraints, dispatch gates); authorizer-emitted receipts; dispatcher-emitted execution events; version-pinned receipts; close the CRX preconditions the checks assume but CRX lacks (causation_id on lifecycle events; wired observability classes; non-empty tables). Residual: paraphrase-level memory use without ID citation is undetectable by set difference.",
 "Low: all eight are indexed anti-joins, existence checks, or grouped queries over the journal; they scale with journal indexing. 1k tenants require tenant in the join keys.",
 "no if every check joins on tenant-scoped keys; yes if any check (especially duplicate-execution grouping or memory-ref sets) aggregates across tenants.")

rec("oa-guardrail-tripwire-audit", "NEEDS-HARDENING",
 "Hung sibling: an output guardrail trips; a sibling guardrail is stuck in an infinite loop. The runner awaits the cancelled sibling with no bound stated, so the tripwire exception never propagates and the run continues past a tripped guardrail. Sink outage: the results sink's database is down; the tripwire still raises (safe), but the tripping record is lost with no typed gap event, so the audit trail has a silent hole exactly when it matters. Runner bypass: a worker invokes the tool directly, bypassing the SDK runner; no guardrail ever runs, because tripwires protect only the paths that invoke them. Catch-and-continue: a higher layer catches the tripwire exception and retries it as a transient failure; the fail-closed becomes a retry loop. Secret excerpts: the tripped result carries an input excerpt containing a secret into the sink, which is exported to the observability vendor (the record admits guardrail results may carry excerpts).",
 "A tripped guardrail halts the run via a typed, non-retryable exception; sibling guardrails are cancelled and awaited under a bound (a hung sibling is killed and logged, never allowed to delay the trip); every completed guardrail result is sink-appended before propagation, and sink failure is itself a loud typed gap event that never un-trips the guardrail; guardrails are enforced at the tool boundary (server side), not only in the agent runner; result excerpts are redacted before sink.",
 "Bounded sibling await with kill; tripwire exceptions excluded from retry by type; server-side guardrail enforcement at the tool boundary; redaction of excerpts before sink; sink-failure gap events; map tripwire types onto CRX typed failures.",
 "Low: guardrails run concurrently per stage; check latency is the max, not the sum.",
 "no if the sink is tenant-scoped and excerpts are redacted; yes if guardrail results from one tenant are visible in another tenant's audit view.")

